<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hadad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Haddu, Adad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hadad was the storm and weather god of the ancient Semitic peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, known in Akkadian as Adad. Attested as Hadda in the archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> in the 3rd millennium BCE, he was worshipped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, where he was later identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>. His symbols were the bull and the thunderbolt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hadad.jpg" alt="Hadad" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by Henri Faucher-Gudin after Austen Henry Layard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramman.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hadad was introduced from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a>, where he became the Akkadian god Adad. The same logogram, dIM, writes Adad, the Sumerian Iškur and the Hurrian god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teshub">Teshub</a>. Adad's worship became widespread in Mesopotamia after the First Babylonian dynasty. Iškur appears in the god list from Shuruppak but was of far less importance, perhaps because storms and rain were scarce in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, where agriculture depended on irrigation. The gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> and Ninurta also had storm-god features. His consort was the grain goddess Shala, and his Babylonian cult centre was Karkara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Hymns, incantations and votive inscriptions show two aspects of Adad. He brings rain in due season to fertilise the land, and he sends storms that wreak destruction; in hymns the sombre aspect predominates. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> he was developed as a warrior god, and from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i">Tiglath-Pileser I</a> he shared a double sanctuary with Anu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>. With the sun-god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a> he presided over oracles and divination, and the two were addressed as lords of divination.</p><p>At Ugarit the Baal Cycle, composed between 1400 and 1200 BCE, presents Ba'al, also called Hadad, as lord of the sky who governs rain and crops. With two clubs made by the craftsman Kothar-wa-Khasis he strikes down Yamm (Sea) and is supreme. A palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, gold and cedar is built for him, but Mot (Death) overcomes him, until his sister 'Anat cleaves Mot and the sun goddess Shapash fetches Ba'al back.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> period and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> was strongly identified with Set, the god of storms and foreign lands, and the Hyksos worshipped him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> as Set of Avaris. Pharaohs of the 19th Dynasty such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> described their armies as like Baal in battle. In Aram the name Hadad appears in royal names: Hadadezer was the Aramean king defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, and later kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> took the title Ben-Hadad, son of Hadad. A king of Yamhad, in the second millennium BCE, called himself beloved of Hadad, and the god's temple stood in the citadel of Halab, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hadad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the north a native Assyrian ruler, Puzur-Sin, expelled the Amorite dynasty installed under Shamshi-<b>Adad</b> and ended Babylonian influence at Assur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious texts record offerings to many gods, among them Kura, the chief god of the city, the storm god <b>Hadda</b>, Rasap and Ishkhara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Parthians a revival in Assyria, from the 2nd century BCE to the 4th century CE, dedicated temples once more to Ashur, Sin, Shamash, <b>Hadad</b>, Bel and Ishtar.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its great kings ruled through dependent kingdoms such as Alalakh in the Kingdom of Mukish and Ebla, and their prestige rested on the temple of the storm god <b>Hadad</b> on the Aleppo citadel.</blockquote><a href="/state/yamhad">Yamhad</a></li><li><blockquote>Ashurnasirpal II campaigned there in the 880s BCE against a coalition led by Nur-<b>Adad</b>, a chief of Dagara, and later against Ameka and Arashtua, destroying fortified towns and imposing tribute of horses, cattle and wine.</blockquote><a href="/state/zamua">Zamua</a></li></ul></section>
